useParams is a react router hook used to get the dynamic parameters from the url.

simple example:
<route path="vendors/:id", element={<Vendors />}
Here :id is a dynamic parameter.

If the URL is:

/vendor/101

Then id will be 101.

Inside the Vendor component:

import { useParams } from "react-router-dom";

function Vendor() {
    const { id } = useParams();

    return <h1>Vendor ID: {id}</h1>;
}

Output:

Vendor ID: 101
How it works

Think of it like:

URL
 ↓
/vendor/101
 ↓
Route
 ↓
/vendor/:id
 ↓
useParams()
 ↓
id = 101
Real-world example

Suppose you have a vendor list:

Vendor A → ID 101
Vendor B → ID 102
Vendor C → ID 103

When the user clicks Vendor A, you can navigate to:

/vendor/101

Then the Vendor Details page can use:

const { id } = useParams();

and use that id to call your Django API:

axios.get(`/api/vendors/${id}/`)
useParams vs useNavigate
Hook	Purpose
useNavigate()	Go to another route
useParams()	Get values from the current URL

For example:

navigate("/vendor/101"); 

